**1. A method for controlling electric power supplied by a power converter, the method comprising:**  
measuring an output current of the power converter;  
calculating, based at least in part on the measured output current, a duty command for switching a switching device of the power converter;  
generating a carrier wave having a carrier-wave cycle;  
setting an operation period during which switching of the switching device is permitted, the operation period being adjusted relative to the carrier-wave cycle; and  
controlling the switching device according to a comparison of the duty command with the carrier wave during the operation period,  
wherein the controlling includes reducing a number of switching transitions in each of a first half cycle and a last half cycle of the carrier wave within the operation period relative to a number of switching transitions in at least one intervening half cycle.

1. **A method for controlling an inverter**, the method comprising:  

   determining whether an operating period of the inverter is to be extended to a duration greater than one carrier cycle;  

   in response to determining that the operating period is to be extended, defining an extended operating period including a first end interval, an intermediate interval, and a second end interval;  

   suppressing switching of at least one switching element of the inverter during the first end interval and the second end interval;  

   varying a slope of a carrier signal during the intermediate interval;  

   comparing the carrier signal having the varied slope with a duty command; and  

   generating, based on a result of the comparison, a switching-control signal for controlling the at least one switching element during the intermediate interval.

1. **A method comprising:**

   controlling, by a controller, an electric power converter having a plurality of switching devices using pulse-width modulation based on a carrier wave and a modulation command;

   designating a modified carrier-wave operation period comprising an initial half cycle, at least one intermediate cycle, and a final half cycle of the carrier wave;

   determining, before the modified carrier-wave operation period, a switching state of the electric power converter immediately preceding the initial half cycle and a target switching state immediately following the final half cycle;

   modifying the carrier wave during the modified carrier-wave operation period by selecting, for the initial half cycle, a first carrier-wave polarity or phase that maintains the switching state immediately preceding the initial half cycle for a maximum available portion of the initial half cycle;

   generating switching commands for the at least one intermediate cycle according to a comparison between the modified carrier wave and the modulation command;

   selecting, for the final half cycle, a second carrier-wave polarity or phase that establishes the target switching state during a maximum available portion of the final half cycle; and

   applying the switching commands to the plurality of switching devices such that a number of switching operations during the initial and final half cycles is less than a number of switching operations that would occur using an unmodified, continuously repeating carrier wave while providing a commanded power-conversion output.

1. **An electric power controller** for controlling transfer of electric power between a power source and a load, the electric power controller comprising:  

   a semiconductor switching circuit including a plurality of semiconductor switches connected in a power-conversion path between the power source and the load;  

   a current sensor configured to measure a current flowing through the power-conversion path and to generate a current-detection value representative of the measured current; and  

   a control circuit configured to:  

   (a) acquire the current-detection value during each of a plurality of control periods;  

   (b) compute, based at least in part on the current-detection value and a target current value, a duty command value specifying an on-time ratio for at least one of the plurality of semiconductor switches;  

   (c) generate a carrier wave having a slope and compare the duty command value with the carrier wave to generate a pulse-width-modulated switching command;  

   (d) operate the plurality of semiconductor switches in accordance with the pulse-width-modulated switching command;  

   (e) determine, based on at least one of the duty command value, the current-detection value, a variation in the current-detection value, an operating state of the semiconductor switching circuit, or a predicted number of switching transitions, whether a presently applicable control period is to be lengthened from a first control-period duration to a second control-period duration greater than the first control-period duration;  

   (f) in response to determining that the presently applicable control period is to be lengthened, change the slope of the carrier wave from a first slope corresponding to the first control-period duration to a second slope corresponding to the second control-period duration, while retaining the duty command value as a representation of the on-time ratio over the second control-period duration; and  

   (g) control generation of the pulse-width-modulated switching command during a first edge interval adjacent a beginning of the second control-period duration and a second edge interval adjacent an end of the second control-period duration so as to reduce a number of switching transitions of the plurality of semiconductor switches within the first and second edge intervals relative to a number of switching transitions that would result from continuously comparing the duty command value with the carrier wave without edge-interval switching reduction,  

   wherein the control circuit reduces the number of switching transitions by performing at least one of suppressing a switching pulse having a duration less than a threshold duration, merging adjacent switching pulses, maintaining a preceding switching state through at least a portion of one of the first and second edge intervals, or shifting a switching transition away from a boundary of the second control-period duration.
